Self-Healing Automation Demo Guide

Project Title:
OpenAI Self-Healing Playwright Automation

1. Objective
The objective of this project is to demonstrate how a test automation pipeline can automatically detect failures, classify them, generate AI-based reports, and recover from known automation issues without manual intervention. It also highlights the difference between automation defects and true application defects.

2. Problem Statement
Modern CI pipelines often fail because of:
- flaky test timing
- outdated selectors
- broken locator references
- real application bugs

In such cases, the team needs a system that can quickly identify the type of failure and take the right action.

3. Solution Overview
This project uses Playwright with a Page Object Model (POM) to structure tests and maintain page logic in reusable classes.

The self-healing layer includes:
- failure detection from Playwright results
- AI-based classification of root causes
- artifact generation for traceability
- rerun of only affected tests
- safe remediation for automation problems
- escalation for genuine product defects

4. Architecture
Core components:
- tests/pipeline-demo.spec.ts
  Contains the business-level test scenarios and pipeline demonstration cases.

- src/pages/login.page.ts
  Stores login selectors and reusable login actions.

- src/pages/inventory.page.ts
  Stores product-page locators and inventory actions.

- agents/self-heal-agent.ts
  Runs the healing workflow and applies approved fixes.

- scripts/self-heal-demo.mjs
  Executes the end-to-end demonstration flow.

- artifacts/
  Stores generated reports:
  - ai-resolution.md
  - defect-report.md
  - healing-summary.md

5. How the Demo Works
Step 1: Run the initial pipeline
Command:
npm run demo:pipeline

This executes the Playwright suite and intentionally fails on selected cases.

Step 2: Detect failures
The framework identifies failed tests such as:
- TC05: Synchronization issue
- TC06: Locator drift
- TC08: Payment control locator issue
- TC10: Application defect (coupon logic issue)

Step 3: Generate AI reports
The system creates the following:
- AI Resolution Report
- Defect Report
- Healing Summary

These files explain what failed, why it failed, and what needs to happen next.

Step 4: Trigger self-healing
Command:
npm run demo:self-heal

This step:
- reads the failed tests
- classifies them
- reruns only the affected ones
- applies approved fixes for automation issues
- keeps application bugs visible for engineering action

6. What the Agent Can Fix vs What It Cannot
The self-healing automation can fix issues such as:
- stale locators
- timing issues
- missing selectors
- short timeout assumptions

It cannot silently hide real product defects. For example:
- incorrect discount logic resulting in a total of 100 instead of 80

In such a case, the system records it as a defect and escalates it to developers instead of weakening the test assertion.

7. Example of a Recoverable Automation Bug
A locator drift issue may occur when a selector changes from an old test id to a new stable selector.

The automation can:
- detect the mismatch
- recommend the new locator
- rerun the relevant test

8. Example of a Real Application Bug
A coupon bug may produce an incorrect total even though the workflow appears to be executing correctly.

This is not a test automation defect. It is an application defect that should be opened as a bug for the development team.

9. Why This Matters
This project illustrates a practical AI-assisted QA approach:
- not every failure is the same
- not every failure should be fixed by changing the assertion
- recoverable automation issues can be healed automatically
- real product defects must still be escalated to developers

10. Final Summary
This project demonstrates how an AI-driven self-healing framework can improve test resilience in CI/CD pipelines by:
- identifying failures quickly
- classifying the root cause
- creating traceable reports
- recovering automation issues automatically
- protecting quality standards by escalating genuine app defects

This is a strong hackathon-ready story because it combines:
- Playwright automation
- Page Object Model structure
- AI-assisted triage
- GitHub Actions artifact generation
- self-healing pipeline behavior
